Do a full, read-only code review of this project. Do not modify any code.

Scope
- Cover the entire application: backend services, data/repository layer, external
  integrations, frontend, browser/extension surfaces, and the test suite.
- Exclude only generated/vendor artifacts: dependency directories (node_modules, .venv,
  etc.), build/dist output, lock files, and already-applied migrations.
- If the invocation names specific areas, issue types, or prior findings to skip, honor
  that exclusion for this run. Absent an explicit exclusion, nothing is out of scope by
  default, including issues found in previous reviews.

Standards
- Read this project's CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md to find which standards files it references
  (e.g. COMMON_CODING_STANDARDS.md plus the language/framework-specific files it lists:
  Python, Flask/FastAPI, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, database, testing, infrastructure,
  Google API, etc.) and apply all of them.
- If any standards file says another standards file must be read first or also applied,
  read and apply that transitive standards file too.

Carry forward unresolved issues (do this before looking for anything new)
- Find this project's prior review docs (e.g. docs/code-review-*.md, or whatever naming
  convention the project already uses) and list every finding they recorded.
- For each one, re-check the current code to see if it was actually fixed:
  - If fixed, drop it (do not report resolved findings).
  - If not fixed, re-report it in the new review, carrying its original "open since" date
    forward, unless this invocation explicitly excluded that issue.
- Never let a real, still-present issue quietly disappear just because a previous review
  already mentioned it. Repetition across reviews is the signal that it needs attention,
  not a reason to stop reporting it.

What to look for (new findings)
- Correctness bugs and edge cases.
- Exception handling: bare `except Exception` outside a request/worker boundary, swallowed
  errors, raw exception text leaked to clients.
- Resource leaks: unclosed files/connections/handles, missing `with`, uncleared
  intervals/timeouts, unbounded in-memory state.
- Dead code, stub handlers, unreachable branches, half-finished implementations.
- Duplicated logic that belongs in a shared helper/service.
- Security: injection (SQL, query-language, prompt injection), SSRF in URL fetchers,
  missing/weak auth, secrets committed to code, unsafe deserialization.
- Test coverage: missing tests for new or changed functionality, and whether the existing
  suite currently passes.
- Efficiency issues: N+1 queries, redundant re-renders, redundant API/network calls.

Verification (required, every run)
- Actually run the project's test suite and any build/lint steps. Report real pass/fail
  counts and warnings. Never describe something as tested or verified without having run it.
- Run ESLint for any JavaScript/TypeScript/React surface when ESLint is configured. If
  ESLint is missing or not configured, report that explicitly rather than treating a
  production build as a substitute for linting.

Output
- Write to this project's existing review-doc location and naming convention if one
  exists; otherwise create docs/code-review-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md (append -2, -3, ... if a file
  for today already exists).
- One heading per finding: Severity (High/Medium/Low), Location (file:line), Impact,
  Evidence, Plan. Mark carried-forward findings with "Open since <original date>".
  Order all findings — new and carried-forward — most severe first.
- Only include findings verified against the actual current code — no speculative or
  unverifiable claims.
- End with a Verification section (actual test/build/lint results) and a Remediation Plan
  numbered by priority, covering both new and carried-forward findings.
